BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20260626T230000Z
DTEND:20260627T010000Z
LOCATION:Recital Hall
SUMMARY:Maya Kilburn\, violin\; Ting-Ting Yang\, piano
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Brahms: Sonata No. 2 in A Major\, Op. 100 (1886)C
 anal: Sonata (1922)Szymanowski: Nocturne and Tarantella\, Op. 28 (1915)Ab
 out the Artists  Violinist Maya Kilburn is a rising artist and recent gra
 duate of The Juilliard School\, where she studied with Donald Weilerstein
  and Catherine Cho as a Kovner Fellow. Originally from Muncie\, Indiana\,
  she began her musical training with Chin Mi Kim and Anna Vayman before c
 ontinuing her studies with Mimi Zweig at the IU Jacobs School of Music St
 ring Academy. She is now a recipient of the Fulbright/Harriet Hale Woolle
 y Award for the Arts in Paris. Kilburn has appeared as a concerto soloist
  with orchestras across the United States and abroad\, including the Indi
 anapolis Symphony\, Fort Wayne Philharmonic\, Louisville Symphony\, Carme
 l Symphony\, PRISMA Festival Orchestra\, and the Orquesta de la Universid
 ad Nacional de Costa Rica. Her performances have taken her to major venue
 s including Carnegie Hall's Stern Auditorium and the Kennedy Center as we
 ll as on tours throughout Argentina\, Chile\, Japan\, and Central America
 . She served regularly as concertmaster of the Juilliard Orchestra and ha
 s also appeared with the New York Philharmonic\, Orpheus Chamber Orchestr
 a\, and Jupiter Symphony Chamber Players. Deeply committed to social impa
 ct through music\, Kilburn has performed alongside Juilliard faculty memb
 ers Catherine Cho and Natasha Brofsky for If Music Be the Food\, a concer
 t series supporting communities experiencing food insecurity. She was a v
 olunteer performer for Brave House\, a New York City organization support
 ing immigrant women. As a Gluck Community Service Fellow (2023-24)\, she 
 brought performances to hospitals\, care centers\, and community organiza
 tions across New York City. She recently appeared at King's Place\, Londo
 n\, in the We Have One World fundraiser for Save the Children UK. An acti
 ve educator with six years of private teaching experience\, Kilburn is pa
 ssionate about mentoring the next generation of artists. She has served a
 s a recurring guest artist and educator at the Universidad Nacional de Co
 sta Rica and La Castella Arts School\, presenting performances and master
  classes and recently taught a guest master class at the University of Al
 berta. Kilburn performs on a 1710 Alessandro Mezzadri violin from Ferrara
 \, Italy\, generously on a 10-year loan.Ting-Ting Yang is a collaborative
  pianist and multigenre composer from Taiwan whose artistry spans perform
 ance\, composition\, and education. She currently serves as associate ins
 tructor at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music\, pursuing a Doc
 tor of Music degree in Collaborative Piano under Anne Epperson and Kevin 
 Murphy. In 2024\, Yang was staff pianist at the Thailand International Tr
 ombone Festival\, collaborating with such world-class trombonists as Jose
 ph Alessi and Brian Hecht\, and pianist-in-residence at Bass Around Asia 
 in Taiwan\, collaborating with such bassists as Artem Chirkov and Julian 
 Li. She was also a Collaborative Piano Fellow at the Bowdoin Internationa
 l Music Festival in 2023 and 2024. Her collaborative highlights include p
 erformances with Gábor Varga (2024) and Musiqa's Twentieth Anniversary C
 oncert (2022)\, recording Anthony Brandt's chamber opera Kassandra (2021)
 \, serving as teaching assistant in collaborative piano at the Brevard Mu
 sic Festival (2022)\, and accompanying the Preparatory Program at Rice Un
 iversity's Shepherd School of Music (2021-22). An internationally invited
  recital pianist\, Yang has collaborated with musicians across Taiwan\, S
 ingapore\, Thailand\, and the United States. As a composer\, Yang began h
 er studies under Ching-Yu Hsiau in Taiwan and graduated with highest dist
 inction from the Yong Siew Toh Conservatory of Music (Singapore)\, where 
 she studied composition under Chee Kong Ho on a full scholarship. Her awa
 rd-winning orchestral composition Portal (2019) received its world premie
 re by the East Carolina University Orchestra and its Asian premiere by th
 e YST Conservatory Orchestra (Singapore). During the COVID-19 pandemic\, 
 Yang engaged in virtual collaborations with such artists as Jon Dante (pr
 incipal trumpet of the Singapore Symphony Orchestra)\, pipa player Li-Ten
 g Huang\, and violist Lauren Ross\, producing works including Floating Rh
 yme (2021) and Drift Along (2022). She also cofounded the H-Tone Electroa
 coustic Ensemble in 2021 to explore multimedia and electroacoustic music.
  Her music\, often inspired by Taiwanese culture\, has been performed in 
 prestigious venues such as Taiwan's National Concert Hall and Taipei Zhon
 gshan Hall.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Repertoire</strong><br />  Brahms
 : <em>Sonata No. 2 in A Major\, Op. 100 (1886)<br /></em>Canal: <em>Sonat
 a (1922)<br /></em>Szymanowski: <em>Nocturne and Tarantella\, Op. 28 (191
 5)</em><br /><br /><strong>About the Artists</strong><br /><br />  Violin
 ist <strong>Maya Kilburn</strong> is a rising artist and recent graduate 
 of The Juilliard School\, where she studied with Donald Weilerstein and C
 atherine Cho as a Kovner Fellow. Originally from Muncie\, Indiana\, she b
 egan her musical training with Chin Mi Kim and Anna Vayman before continu
 ing her studies with Mimi Zweig at the IU Jacobs School of Music String A
 cademy. She is now a recipient of the Fulbright/Harriet Hale Woolley Awar
 d for the Arts in Paris. Kilburn has appeared as a concerto soloist with 
 orchestras across the United States and abroad\, including the Indianapol
 is Symphony\, Fort Wayne Philharmonic\, Louisville Symphony\, Carmel Symp
 hony\, PRISMA Festival Orchestra\, and the Orquesta de la Universidad Nac
 ional de Costa Rica. Her performances have taken her to major venues incl
 uding Carnegie Hall's Stern Auditorium and the Kennedy Center as well as 
 on tours throughout Argentina\, Chile\, Japan\, and Central America. She 
 served regularly as concertmaster of the Juilliard Orchestra and has also
  appeared with the New York Philharmonic\, Orpheus Chamber Orchestra\, an
 d Jupiter Symphony Chamber Players. Deeply committed to social impact thr
 ough music\, Kilburn has performed alongside Juilliard faculty members Ca
 therine Cho and Natasha Brofsky for If Music Be the Food\, a concert seri
 es supporting communities experiencing food insecurity. She was a volunte
 er performer for Brave House\, a New York City organization supporting im
 migrant women. As a Gluck Community Service Fellow (2023-24)\, she brough
 t performances to hospitals\, care centers\, and community organizations 
 across New York City. She recently appeared at King's Place\, London\, in
  the We Have One World fundraiser for Save the Children UK. An active edu
 cator with six years of private teaching experience\, Kilburn is passiona
 te about mentoring the next generation of artists. She has served as a re
 curring guest artist and educator at the Universidad Nacional de Costa Ri
 ca and La Castella Arts School\, presenting performances and master class
 es and recently taught a guest master class at the University of Alberta.
  Kilburn performs on a 1710 Alessandro Mezzadri violin from Ferrara\, Ita
 ly\, generously on a 10-year loan.<br /><br /><strong>Ting-Ting Yang</str
 ong> is a collaborative pianist and multigenre composer from Taiwan whose
  artistry spans performance\, composition\, and education. She currently 
 serves as associate instructor at the Indiana University Jacobs School of
  Music\, pursuing a Doctor of Music degree in Collaborative Piano under A
 nne Epperson and Kevin Murphy. In 2024\, Yang was staff pianist at the Th
 ailand International Trombone Festival\, collaborating with such world-cl
 ass trombonists as Joseph Alessi and Brian Hecht\, and pianist-in-residen
 ce at Bass Around Asia in Taiwan\, collaborating with such bassists as Ar
 tem Chirkov and Julian Li. She was also a Collaborative Piano Fellow at t
 he Bowdoin International Music Festival in 2023 and 2024. Her collaborati
 ve highlights include performances with Gábor Varga (2024) and Musiqa's 
 Twentieth Anniversary Concert (2022)\, recording Anthony Brandt's chamber
  opera <em>Kassandra</em> (2021)\, serving as teaching assistant in colla
 borative piano at the Brevard Music Festival (2022)\, and accompanying th
 e Preparatory Program at Rice University's Shepherd School of Music (2021
 -22). An internationally invited recital pianist\, Yang has collaborated 
 with musicians across Taiwan\, Singapore\, Thailand\, and the United Stat
 es. As a composer\, Yang began her studies under Ching-Yu Hsiau in Taiwan
  and graduated with highest distinction from the Yong Siew Toh Conservato
 ry of Music (Singapore)\, where she studied composition under Chee Kong H
 o on a full scholarship. Her award-winning orchestral composition <em>Por
 tal</em> (2019) received its world premiere by the East Carolina Universi
 ty Orchestra and its Asian premiere by the YST Conservatory Orchestra (Si
 ngapore). During the COVID-19 pandemic\, Yang engaged in virtual collabor
 ations with such artists as Jon Dante (principal trumpet of the Singapore
  Symphony Orchestra)\, pipa player Li-Teng Huang\, and violist Lauren Ros
 s\, producing works including <em>Floating Rhyme</em> (2021) and <em>Drif
 t Along</em> (2022). She also cofounded the H-Tone Electroacoustic Ensemb
 le in 2021 to explore multimedia and electroacoustic music. Her music\, o
 ften inspired by Taiwanese culture\, has been performed in prestigious ve
 nues such as Taiwan's National Concert Hall and Taipei Zhongshan Hall.</p
 >
